  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The evaluation was demonstrated on a single driving scenario involving a cyclist overtaking manoeuvre under synthetic contextual perturbations.",
      "what": "The authors developed CARE-Drive, a method to test whether vision-language models (VLMs) alter their driving decisions based on specific contextual inputs rather than generating post-hoc rationalizations. Tested using GPT-4.1 on a simulated cyclist overtaking scenario, the framework uses a two-stage process that screens model configurations for consistency and measures decision shifts under varied safety, social pressure, and efficiency conditions. The model demonstrated context-sensitive decision shifts under full prompting, but its sensitivity was uneven across specific variables, such as time-to-collision, trailing vehicles, passenger urgency, and following time.",
      "why": "The framework provides a way to audit whether autonomous driving foundation models act on human-relevant reasoning without needing access to alter internal model weights."
    }
  },
